# Milestone: M3
# Covers: two strands enqueued FIFO; after a soft yield the first strand
#         goes to the tail and the second strand runs next (FIFO ordering
#         across yield boundaries — row 12 §3.4 single-entry make_runnable
#         invariant).
#
# blocked: cross-strand FIFO ordering across a YIELD boundary not observable
#          via the public API — the chk-host-driver can fork two strands
#          (`,` runs under the realm-managed run_chunk path), but with the
#          compile-time URBI_STRAND_BUDGET_MAX each forked strand runs to
#          completion before yielding, so there is no observable yield point
#          at which to assert tail-of-FIFO requeue order.  Needs a runtime
#          per-strand budget knob to force a mid-strand yield.  v0.10.7-G residual.
# matrix-row: scheduler-fifo
#
# Reason: verifying cross-strand FIFO ordering requires two concurrent
# strands.  The chk runner uses the urbi_vm_run REPL path which has no realm;
# `,` (OP_FORK_DETACH) produces "TypeError: requires urbi_step driver"
# in that context.  The test is substantively covered by test_fork.c
# (fork_detach_basic_no_crash + fork_detach_quiescent_count_zero) at M3.
# A proper chk fixture needs the ## host: driver mechanism (T39-style) to
# create two named strands and verify their scheduling order via output.
#
# Urbiscript source (activate at M5 with urbi_step chk driver):
#   # var a_ran = false | var b_ran = false
#   # mytag: {
#   #   var a = Tag.new | var b = Tag.new
#   #   a: { a_ran = true | yield } ,
#   #   b: { b_ran = true }
#   # }
#   # -- after quiescent --
#   # echo(a_ran)
#   # echo(b_ran)
#   --- expected output (FIFO: a runs first, then b) ---
#   [00000001] true
#   [00000002] true
